#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# ralph-init — scaffold a new Ralph Wiggum loop project in the CURRENT directory.
#
# Drops template prompt.md, spec.md, and implementation_plan.md (it never
# overwrites an existing file unless you pass --force), and adds Ralph's log
# files to .gitignore. The loop script itself (`ralph`) is NOT copied here — it's
# a tool you install once with install.sh and run from any project.
#
# Usage:
#   ralph-init            # scaffold templates (skips files that already exist)
#   ralph-init --force    # overwrite existing template files
#
# Prefer having Claude write a tailored spec for you? In Claude Code, run the
# /ralph-init slash command instead — it interviews you and writes the files.

set -euo pipefail

force=0
[ "${1:-}" = "--force" ] && force=1

scaffold() {  # scaffold <path>   (file body comes from stdin/heredoc)
  local path="$1"
  if [ -e "$path" ] && [ "$force" -ne 1 ]; then
    printf '  skip    %s (exists — use --force to overwrite)\n' "$path"
    cat >/dev/null   # drain the heredoc so it isn't left on stdin
    return
  fi
  cat > "$path"
  printf '  create  %s\n' "$path"
}

echo "Scaffolding a Ralph loop in: $(pwd)"

scaffold prompt.md <<'EOF'
# Ralph — per-iteration prompt

You are ONE iteration of an autonomous build loop. You have a FRESH context: the
only reliable memory of this project lives in `spec.md`, `implementation_plan.md`,
and the repository itself. Those files are the source of truth, not your context.

Do exactly one task, well, then exit.

## Steps
1. Study `spec.md` thoroughly. It is the source of truth for WHAT to build and why.
2. Study `implementation_plan.md` thoroughly. It lists the tasks and their state.
3. Pick the single highest-leverage UNCHECKED task (`- [ ]`). Do ONLY that one.
4. Complete the task, following the repository's existing structure and conventions.
5. Write an unbiased unit test that would genuinely fail if the task were done
   wrong — not one written to pass. Run it.

## Marking completion
- Only if the test passes: change that task's `- [ ]` to `- [x]` in
  `implementation_plan.md`. Add a one-line note beneath it if the next iteration
  needs to know something non-obvious.
- If the spec is ambiguous or contradictory, DO NOT guess — a wrong guess poisons
  every future loop. Record the decision in `spec.md` (or add a `> BLOCKED: ...`
  note under the task) and stop.
- Do NOT start a second task. One task per iteration keeps context small and well
  under the "dumb zone" (~100k tokens), where quality falls off a cliff.

## Repository context & conventions
<!-- TODO: fill this in for THIS project so a fresh context gets up to speed fast. -->
- Language / runtime:
- Test framework:
- Where source lives and how it's organized:
- Conventions the agent must follow (naming, error handling, formatting):
- Anything it must NOT do (add deps, touch certain dirs, etc.):

Now begin: study the specs, pick the highest-leverage unchecked task, and implement it.
EOF

scaffold spec.md <<'EOF'
# Feature: <name>

> Read every line and sign off on every line. If the plan isn't bulletproof,
> errors cascade and amplify across loops.

## Problem
<!-- What can't the user do today? Be concrete. -->

## Goal
<!-- One or two sentences: what does "done" look like? -->

## Requirements
- <!-- concrete, testable requirement -->
- <!-- concrete, testable requirement -->

## Out of scope (v1)
- <!-- explicitly excluded, so no iteration wanders into it -->

## Key decisions & constraints
<!-- Kill implicit assumptions here BEFORE the loop runs. Every ambiguity you
     resolve now (data model, relationships, formats) is a bug that never happens. -->
- Language / runtime / test framework:
- Data model & key relationships:
- <!-- resolved ambiguity, e.g. "accounts are flat, not nested" -->

## Acceptance criteria
- <!-- how we'll know each piece works -->
EOF

scaffold implementation_plan.md <<'EOF'
# Implementation Plan

Write one checkbox line per task below — each unchecked box is exactly one Ralph
iteration. Keep tasks small so a single fresh context can finish one without
approaching the "dumb zone" (~100k tokens), and make each independently testable.
An iteration marks a task done by turning `- [ ]` into `- [x]`, and only after its
unit test passes. `ralph` stops automatically when no unchecked boxes remain — so
this empty plan is a safe no-op that just tells you to add tasks.

Format is a dash, a space, then an empty checkbox. Replace the examples below with
your own (the "e.g." prefix keeps them from counting as real tasks until you do):
  e.g.  - [ ] Create the data models
  e.g.  - [ ] Build the import parser


## Notes for future iterations
_(Ralph appends anything the next pass must know here.)_
EOF

# Make sure Ralph's runtime artifacts are ignored.
touch .gitignore
for entry in "logs/" ".ralph-done"; do
  grep -qxF "$entry" .gitignore 2>/dev/null || printf '%s\n' "$entry" >> .gitignore
done
echo "  update  .gitignore (logs/, .ralph-done)"

cat <<'EOF'

Next:
  1. Fill in spec.md and implementation_plan.md
       (or run /ralph-init in Claude Code to have Claude interview you and write them).
  2. Edit prompt.md's "Repository context & conventions" for this project.
  3. Read every line and sign off.
  4. Start the loop:   ralph        (or ./ralph.sh if not installed globally)
EOF
